account.terms.n1accessandwhatyourplanincludes2accessisbysubsc = { $slot-1 }Access, and what your plan includes.{ $slot-2 } Access is by subscription, or complimentary while you're an approved scout (section 16). When you subscribe, what your plan includes and what happens when you reach it, the price, the billing interval, and the automatic renewal are all shown to you before we take any billing details, and section 3 applies. If we add a limit to a plan you already have, section 9 applies to it as a material change. Reaching what your plan includes can slow your processing or make it wait its turn, but it never ends your access before the end of the period you paid for.
account.terms.n1accessrecordsnotcontent2toruntheserviceandcon = { $slot-1 }Access records, not content.{ $slot-2 } To run the service and control who can use it, we keep three records about access: that you turned it on and acknowledged the disclosure, each time your journal was issued a credential or refused one, and the short-lived record the turn-on page leaves so your journal can collect its credential. None says how much you used, and none says anything about what you sent. The privacy policy lists what each one holds ({ $slot-3 }confidential processing{ $slot-4 }). All of them are Customer Data under our covenants, and all of them are deleted when your sign-in is, apart from what section 7 says outlasts a deletion.
account.terms.n1afterthe72hourssolpbcdeletestheoperatedbackup = { $slot-1 }After the 72 hours, { $slot-2 } deletes the operated backup copy{ $slot-3 }, with no 30-day lapse window. If you've turned on media offload, that copy is the only copy of that media, and it goes too, so restore that media to your devices before you confirm.
account.terms.n1afteryoursubscriptionlapseswekeepyourencrypte = { $slot-1 }After your subscription lapses, we keep your encrypted backup for 30 days, then delete it.{ $slot-2 } Here is the sequence, whether you cancel, withdraw (section 3), a renewal payment fails, or your scout access ends:
account.terms.n1ajournalthatisntreachableisordinary2yourjourn = { $slot-1 }A journal that isn't reachable is ordinary.{ $slot-2 } Your journal lives on a machine that sleeps, travels, and loses wifi, so an agent will often find it unreachable, and should tell you so rather than hang. That isn't a failure of this service, and section 8 is the general position on availability.
account.terms.n1alimitspecifictothisservice2theoperatedcopyis = { $slot-1 }A limit specific to this service.{ $slot-2 } The operated copy is encrypted with a key only you hold, so we can't read it, rebuild it, or put a value on what's in it, and for everything except media you offloaded it's a second copy of a journal that lives on your own devices. So { $slot-3 }{ $slot-4 } is not liable for any inability to restore a copy where you have lost your recovery key, or for what was inside a lost operated copy, except where our own failure to keep it available is what lost it. None of this limits our responsibility to keep your backup available while you're paying for it or have it as a scout, and to keep it for the 30 days after that this section promises{ $slot-5 }, and if we fail at that, section 8 says the most we can owe you.
account.terms.n1amodelsolpbcrunsitself2ourownmodelweightsserv = { $slot-1 }A model { $slot-2 } runs itself.{ $slot-3 } Our own model weights, served by { $slot-4 }, on confidential GPU hardware { $slot-5 } operates. It's the same model generation your own device runs, with more room to run it: more capacity, never a better model. { $slot-6 }No third-party AI provider is in the path{ $slot-7 }, and nothing you send is handed to one.
account.terms.n1arenewalisneverasurprise2onanannualplanweemai = { $slot-1 }A renewal is never a surprise.{ $slot-2 } On an annual plan we email you twice before each renewal: 60 to 70 days before, and again 35 to 40 days before. Each is an email of its own, with the date to cancel by set out in a box. On a monthly plan we email you 25 to 28 days before the renewal at each six-month mark. Each says when it renews, what it will cost, what you paid last time, and how to cancel, and by when. Colorado's automatic renewal law (C.R.S. § 6-1-732(4)) and the laws of some other places require them.
account.terms.n1asecondcopyunlessyouchooseotherwise2anencrypt = { $slot-1 }A second copy, unless you choose otherwise.{ $slot-2 } An encrypted backup is a second copy of a journal that lives on your own devices, not a substitute for it. There is one exception, and it's your choice: media offload is off unless you turn it on, and if you do, your journal removes media from your device once the backup holds it, and for that media the backup is the only copy. Everything in this section, including what happens after a lapse, applies to that media too. If we ever lost that copy, that media would be gone, and section 8's limit is what we'd owe you; so don't let the backup go while it holds the only copy of something you want.
account.terms.n1blindbyconstruction2therelaypassesencryptedby = { $slot-1 }Blind by construction.{ $slot-2 } The relay passes encrypted bytes between your devices. It has no key to read them and keeps no copy of what flows through. We operate the relay and cannot read what it carries.
account.terms.n1blindbyconstructionandthisrelayholdsnostateab = { $slot-1 }Blind by construction, and this relay holds no state about you.{ $slot-2 } Your own machine holds the private key and ends the encryption, so the { $slot-3 } relay has no key and cannot read a byte of what passes through it. It also keeps nothing: no database and no record of what passes through it. That is how it is built, not a policy we apply to it, and what it does write down about its own health says that something happened, never who it happened to. The machine it runs on is { $slot-4 }'s, and Azure is not a party to your content.
account.terms.n1cancelanytimewithnomorestepsthanittooktosubsc = { $slot-1 }Cancel anytime, with no more steps than it took to subscribe.{ $slot-2 } Sign in at { $slot-3 } and use { $slot-4 }cancel contracts here{ $slot-5 }, on the service's page or at { $slot-6 }. Check what you're canceling and the day it ends, then press { $slot-7 }cancel now{ $slot-8 }. We email you straight away with what you sent, when we received it, and the day your subscription ends. You can also email { $slot-9 } saying you cancel, in any words, and we reply with the same record (inside your 14 days, the withdrawal record). Within your 14 days to withdraw (below), an email saying you cancel counts as a withdrawal, unless you tell us to keep the service running. No phone call, no retention maze. If you're canceling for a serious reason, such as something we did wrong, say so on the page and tell us why. You can also choose to end it that day, and we'll answer you about refunding the time you've paid for after it. The prorated mid-term exit in section 9 is the one you ask for by email only.
account.terms.n1complimentarymeanscomplimentary2whileyoureana = { $slot-1 }Complimentary means complimentary.{ $slot-2 } While you're an approved scout, you aren't charged for those services and sections 3 and 4 don't apply to them. Everything else does.
account.terms.n1encryptedbyconstructiononlyyoucanreadit2befor = { $slot-1 }Encrypted by construction: only you can read it.{ $slot-2 } Before anything leaves your device, solstone encrypts it, so the contents, the file names, and the folder structure all become unreadable ciphertext. We store those encrypted blocks and { $slot-3 }cannot read them{ $slot-4 }: we hold no key, no password, and no way to decrypt your backup.
account.terms.n1everythingisshownbeforeyoupay2eachservicehasa = { $slot-1 }Everything is shown before you pay.{ $slot-2 } Each service has a flat price, tax included, per journal, not per device: one subscription covers one journal and every device you've paired with it (connected to it as one of yours). The price, the billing interval, that it renews automatically, and how to cancel are all shown to you at checkout, before we take any billing details, and you agree to it there before any charge. After you subscribe we send you a written confirmation you can keep, with those renewal terms, our cancellation policy, and how to cancel. Colorado's automatic renewal law requires each of these (C.R.S. § 6-1-732(2)). Checkout and that confirmation also tell you how to withdraw (below), and the confirmation carries the withdrawal form.
account.terms.n1fairusespecifically2confidentialprocessingisf = { $slot-1 }Fair use, specifically.{ $slot-2 } Confidential processing is for thinking with your own journal. Don't use it to generate or pursue things that are unlawful, that are meant to harm or harass someone, that impersonate a real person in order to deceive, or that attack the service or anyone else's systems.
account.terms.n1fairusespecifically2theoperatedtierisforbacki = { $slot-1 }Fair use, specifically.{ $slot-2 } The operated tier is for backing up your own journal. Don't use it to store or distribute anything else.
account.terms.n1fairusespecifically2thisisforyourownagentsrea = { $slot-1 }Fair use, specifically.{ $slot-2 } This is for your own agents reading your own journal. Don't use the address to run a service for other people, to route traffic that isn't yours, or to serve anything other than your journal.
account.terms.n1howitworks2yousigninwithyouremailwesendyouaco = { $slot-1 }How it works.{ $slot-2 } You sign in with your email (we send you a code) or with a passkey (a sign-in key your own device holds, so there's no password to make up or lose). A session lasts up to two weeks unless you sign out sooner, and you can end any session from the portal.
account.terms.n1ifsomethinggoeswrongwhethertheclaimisaboutase = { $slot-1 }If something goes wrong, whether the claim is about a service, the portal, your sign-in, or scout, the most we owe you is what you paid us for that service in the past year, or $100, whichever is more{ $slot-2 }, except where the law says we can't limit it, and except for the covenants in section 6, our promise never to train a model on what you send us, section 13's promise that nothing you send is kept, and section 14's promise that we hold no readable record of what your agents asked, were shown, or were refused, none of which are capped at all. Encrypted backup has one rule of its own: keeping your copy available while you pay (or have it as a scout), and keeping it for the 30 days after, is on us; what was inside a lost copy, which we can't read or rebuild, is not, unless our own failure to keep it available is what lost it (section 12).
account.terms.n1ifyoupayitrenewsuntilyoucancelandcancelingisa = { $slot-1 }If you pay, it renews until you cancel, and canceling is as easy as subscribing.{ $slot-2 } Turning a service off doesn't cancel its subscription. Using { $slot-3 }cancel contracts here{ $slot-4 } does, and so does an email saying you cancel; inside your 14 days to withdraw, that email counts as a withdrawal (section 3). You keep what you paid for through the end of the period. Once an annual plan has renewed, a cancel ends it at the end of your current plan month, and we refund the whole months left. { $slot-5 }For 14 days after you buy a subscription, and for 14 days after each renewal of an annual plan, you can withdraw for a full refund, wherever you live{ $slot-6 } (section 3). If we ever charge you by mistake, we refund it.
account.terms.n1ifyoustoppayingforencryptedbackupwekeeptheenc = { $slot-1 }If you stop paying for encrypted backup, we keep the encrypted copy for 30 days after your paid period or your access ends, then delete it for good.{ $slot-2 } If you offloaded media into it, that copy is the only one.
account.terms.n1insidethecontainer2theservicekeepsthecountofh = { $slot-1 }Inside the container{ $slot-2 }, the service keeps the count of how much confidential processing it has done for a journal, so it can manage capacity, and it notes that a channel was admitted or refused. That count is keyed to your credential, not to your sign-in, and neither it nor those notes record what you sent or where you connected from.
account.terms.n1itcanend2scoutstatusisourstograntandourstoend = { $slot-1 }It can end.{ $slot-2 } Scout status is ours to grant and ours to end, and we can end it at any time as ordinary management of the program. Ending it doesn't close your sign-in: it ends the free access, and anything you pay for separately keeps running; you can always subscribe to a service you were getting as a scout. If encrypted backup was on for you as a scout, section 12's 30-day clock starts the day your access ends. Once your access has ended your journal can't read the backup until you subscribe, so if you offloaded media, subscribing within those 30 days is the way to keep it. If { $slot-3 } was on for you as a scout, your journal stops being reachable through the { $slot-4 } relay when your access ends, and your address stays reserved for you on the same terms as section 14 sets out.
account.terms.n1itnevertouches2yourjournalonyourdevicesyourde = { $slot-1 }It never touches{ $slot-2 } your journal on your devices, your devices themselves, or a bucket you control.
account.terms.n1itsprotecteddifferentlyfromourotherthreeservi = { $slot-1 }It's protected differently from our other three services.{ $slot-2 } The private network relay and the { $slot-3 } relay can't read what passes through them, and the operated backup holds blocks we have no key to. This one runs inside a { $slot-4 }confidential container{ $slot-5 }: a machine whose hardware walls off what's in its memory from the company that hosts it. What you send is encrypted over the network and visible in running memory only while it's being processed: { $slot-6 }our own model reads what you send, in the clear, while it answers you.{ $slot-7 } We can't promise "no key" here, so instead your journal checks a fingerprint of the exact image it booted before it sends anything.
account.terms.n1itstopstheservicesonyoursigninassoonasyouconf = { $slot-1 }It stops the services on your sign-in as soon as you confirm.{ $slot-2 } None of them starts anything new for you from then on. What's already under way can finish, and three things can take a while: a backup or restore already running, a confidential processing connection that's already open (section 13), and a { $slot-3 } address, which can keep working until the short-lived pass your journal already holds for it runs out (section 14).
account.terms.n1itsyourstokeepsafe2asigninisforonepersonkeepy = { $slot-1 }It's yours to keep safe.{ $slot-2 } A sign-in is for one person. Keep your passkeys and your email in your own hands: anyone who can read your email or use your passkey can sign in as you and manage your services. If you think that's happened, sign out everywhere from the portal and tell us. The privacy policy says when one of our operators can open a session on your sign-in, and how you see and end it.
account.terms.n1notatrackingsurface2noanalyticsnobehavioralpr = { $slot-1 }Not a tracking surface.{ $slot-2 } No analytics, no behavioral profiling, and no third party in the path but your phone's push service and, for iphone, { $slot-3 }, whose network our hop runs on. Notifications never become a way to watch you: the profiling half of that is Article 8, and the rest is a promise we make here.
account.terms.n1nothinghereisrequired2thesolstonesoftwareruns = { $slot-1 }Nothing here is required.{ $slot-2 } The solstone software runs on devices you own, and your journal lives on one of them. Four services take money today: private network, encrypted backup, confidential processing, and { $slot-3 }. Each has a way to do the same thing with { $slot-4 } out of the path. Notifications to your phone are off until you turn them on. These terms cover your sign-in, the portal, and the parts { $slot-5 } runs for you when you turn them on.
account.terms.n1nothingonyourdevicesislostwhenaservicestopswi = { $slot-1 }Nothing on your devices is lost when a service stops, with one exception.{ $slot-2 } Your journal, your devices, and your device pairings are untouched. The exception is media you offloaded into encrypted backup, which lives only there. What happens to a copy or a credential { $slot-3 } held is in that service's section, and if you've turned on media offload, section 12 is the one to read before you let backup lapse. Re-subscribe anytime to turn a service back on.
account.terms.n1nothingyousendisusedtotrainanything2notourmod = { $slot-1 }Nothing you send is used to train anything.{ $slot-2 } Not our models, not anyone else's. This is a commitment we make to you, and it's reinforced by the covenants in section 6.
account.terms.n1onthedeviceandonyourphone2aheadsupcanbealocal = { $slot-1 }On the device, and on your phone.{ $slot-2 } A heads-up can be a local notification on the device that shows it, and that never leaves the device. Notifications from your journal can also reach your phone when you're away from home. Those are off until you turn them on, phone by phone, in the solstone app, and your journal locks each one to a key only that phone and your journal hold before it leaves your machine.
