account.terms.n1youcanalwaysturnaserviceoffandwhatsolpbcrunsf = { $slot-1 }You can always turn a service off, and what { $slot-2 } runs for you is nearly all that changes.{ $slot-3 } Two things to know, and the first is the one exception: media you offload into encrypted backup lives only there, so letting backup go takes that media with it (section 12); and closing your sign-in ends a paid subscription and deletes the operated backup copy with it, with no 30 days (section 7). Apart from offloaded media, nothing on your devices changes.
account.terms.n1youcanwithdrawwithin14daysforafullrefundwhere = { $slot-1 }You can withdraw within 14 days, for a full refund, wherever you live.{ $slot-2 } Until the end of the 14th day after the day you buy a subscription, you can withdraw from it, even if you've already started using the service. On an annual plan you can withdraw again after each renewal, until the end of the 14th day after the day you pay for it. Use { $slot-3 }withdraw from contract here{ $slot-4 } on that service's page or at { $slot-5 }, and confirm; or email { $slot-6 } saying you withdraw, with or without the form below. A withdrawal you send within the 14 days counts, even if it reaches us later. Withdrawing ends the service that day, as if its paid period had ended, and each service's section says what that means for it. We refund everything you paid for that subscription, or after a renewal everything you paid for that renewal, within 14 days, the same way you paid. If you withdraw on the page, we email you a record of your withdrawal right away; if you email us, we reply to confirm it. A monthly renewal doesn't start a new 14 days. We start a service as soon as you pay, so the page you subscribe from asks you to confirm that's what you want; confirming doesn't change any of this. The laws of some places, including the EU and the UK, give you a right to withdraw too, and this doesn't take it away.
account.terms.n1youhave72hourstochangeyourmind2keepyoursignin = { $slot-1 }You have 72 hours to change your mind.{ $slot-2 } Keep your sign-in before they're up, and your services come back on, as your subscriptions and scout access allow. Nothing renews while your sign-in is closing. If a subscription was due to renew in that time, keeping your sign-in charges that renewal then, and its new period starts that day. The time your services were stopped isn't refunded or added on.
account.terms.n1youneverhavetopayus2pointsolstoneatyourownbuc = { $slot-1 }You never have to pay us.{ $slot-2 } Point solstone at your own bucket (Backblaze B2, Amazon S3, { $slot-3 } R2, any S3-compatible provider), pay that provider directly, and { $slot-4 } is never contacted and never holds your data. The bring-your-own path and the operated tier use the { $slot-5 }same engine, the same encryption, and the same recovery model{ $slot-6 }; the only difference is whose bucket the encrypted blocks land in. { $slot-7 }The operated tier is convenience, never a privacy upgrade.{ $slot-8 }
account.terms.n1youneverhavetopayus2thesameprivateconnectioni = { $slot-1 }You never have to pay us.{ $slot-2 } The same private connection is always available for free. On the same network, your devices connect to your journal directly. Or point solstone at your own VPN, Tailscale, or tunnel. Or run the open-source relay yourself. All three are private by the same construction as the relay we run, because your devices encrypt to each other whatever path they take. { $slot-3 }The relay is convenience, never a privacy upgrade.{ $slot-4 }
account.terms.n1youneverhavetopayus2thisiscapacitynotagateyou = { $slot-1 }You never have to pay us.{ $slot-2 } This is capacity, not a gate. Your journal can always think without us: with a model on your own hardware, where nothing leaves your device and { $slot-3 } is not in the path; with your own provider key, where the key stays in your journal and { $slot-4 } is not in the path; or with any endpoint you run or trust. { $slot-5 }Confidential processing is never a privacy upgrade over running locally.{ $slot-6 } If you stop using it, you lose capacity. You don't lose your journal and you don't lose your privacy.
account.terms.n1youneverhavetopayus2yourjournaldoesntneedsolp = { $slot-1 }You never have to pay us.{ $slot-2 } Your journal doesn't need { $slot-3 } to be reachable. A tunnel that only passes the bytes through works today with nothing of ours in the path, whether you rent one or run your own on a machine you control. One warning: { $slot-4 }some free tunnels decrypt your traffic in order to move it.{ $slot-5 } Whoever runs one of those can read what your agent reads, and can reuse your agent's key to reach your journal as though they were your agent. Look for a tunnel that says it only passes the bytes through; if its documentation doesn't say, assume it ends the encryption. { $slot-6 }The { $slot-7 } relay is convenience, never a privacy upgrade over a tunnel that only passes the bytes through.{ $slot-8 }
account.terms.n1youraddressisneveranyoneelses2anaddressoncemi = { $slot-1 }Your address is never anyone else's.{ $slot-2 } An address, once minted, is never issued to another journal. We keep the reservation that makes that true with no name, sign-in, or journal attached to it, which is why it can outlive your sign-in without saying anything about you (section 7). Turning the service off keeps your address, and turning it back on uses the same one, with no new certificate while the one you have is still good, so your agents go on working without being set up again. { $slot-3 }The same holds if you stop paying:{ $slot-4 } your journal stops being reachable through the { $slot-5 } relay when your subscription ends (section 3), your address stays reserved for you, and subscribing again brings back the same one. Minting you a second address would add permanent records for that address to those public logs and break every agent you'd connected, and it would take nothing back, because the records for the first address never go away.
account.terms.n1yourjournalchecksbeforeitsends2beforeanything = { $slot-1 }Your journal checks before it sends.{ $slot-2 } Before anything is sent, your journal verifies the hardware, and a fingerprint of the exact image it booted, against a fingerprint pinned in solstone's open source code. (In full: the AMD attestation chain up to AMD's own signing keys, the GPU's own evidence, the binding of that evidence to the encrypted connection, and the boot fingerprint.) The pin is public and version-controlled, and it ships in the same releases everything else does. { $slot-3 }We commit that { $slot-4 } will not point you at different software without a release you can read.{ $slot-5 } If the check fails, nothing is sent: your journal waits, tells you plainly that it couldn't verify, and never silently falls back to another service or another provider.
account.terms.n1yourjournalsaddressandthepartofitthatisperman = { $slot-1 }Your journal's address, and the part of it that is permanent.{ $slot-2 } When you turn this on, { $slot-3 } mints an address for your journal: eight random characters with nothing of yours in them, followed by { $slot-4 }. Your journal then requests a real certificate for that address itself, the same kind secure websites use, and its key never leaves your machine; that certificate is how your agent can tell it's really your journal at the other end. Issuing and renewing a certificate can be delayed, and there is a weekly ceiling on new certificates across everyone we serve, so turning on can land in a waiting state and a renewal can run late; the journal shows you which, and keeps trying. If an address never issues at all, write to { $slot-5 } and we refund what you paid. { $slot-6 }One thing about that address is public, and stays that way.{ $slot-7 } Like the certificates behind secure websites, each certificate your journal gets for your address is listed in public certificate logs that nobody can edit, and a new entry is added each time one is issued or renewed. An entry shows that the address exists. None says whose, though an agent you connect knows the address is yours, and so do we until your sign-in is deleted. It stays if you turn this off, cancel, or close your sign-in, and nobody can remove it, including us. The authority your journal asks for the certificate is today Let's Encrypt, and the privacy policy says the same ({ $slot-8 }{ $slot-9 }{ $slot-10 }).
account.terms.n1yourrecoverykeyistheonlykey2whenyousetupbacku = { $slot-1 }Your recovery key is the only key.{ $slot-2 } When you set up backup, solstone gave you a recovery key. A backup is restored only with it, and we don't have it. Keep it safe. If you lose it, the backup can't be restored, by you or by us.
account.terms.n1yourrightsasaconsumer2ifaserviceisntasdescrib = { $slot-1 }Your rights as a consumer.{ $slot-2 } If a service isn't as described or doesn't work, the law where you live may give you rights about it: in the EU, the legal guarantee of conformity; in the UK, the Consumer Rights Act 2015. Nothing in these terms limits them.
account.terms.n1yoursubscriptionrenewsautomatically2attheendo = { $slot-1 }Your subscription renews automatically{ $slot-2 } at the end of each term, once a year on the annual plan or once a month on the monthly plan, at your plan's then-current price, using the payment method on file, { $slot-3 }until you cancel{ $slot-4 }. Nothing renews while your sign-in is closing (section 7).
account.terms.n1youruseismeteredinsidethecontainer2theservice = { $slot-1 }Your use is metered, inside the container.{ $slot-2 } The service keeps a count of how much confidential processing it has done for a journal, so it can manage capacity. Apart from being down or full for a moment like any service (section 8), it can slow you down or say no when your access has ended, or briefly under fair use, where a no is temporary and clears on its own. Your journal keeps thinking on its own either way. { $slot-3 }Being metered is not being suspended{ $slot-4 }: a limit doesn't reach your journal, your local processing, or your own key or endpoint, and the other paths above are always there.
